"""Original community adaptation planning teaching."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-044","Community Adaptation and Resilience Planning","community-adaptation-and-resilience-planning","Community Adaptation\nand Resilience Planning",
        "Develop a feasible community adaptation plan with explicit assumptions, responsibilities, operating resources and a proportionate review trigger.",[
m("Outcomes options and possible harm","Compare adaptation options against a stated participation outcome, including burdens and assumptions.",[
"An adaptation plan begins with a specific problem and a desired change, using an assessment that people can examine. State whose participation or access should improve and under what conditions. Distinguish an output, such as an agreed contact arrangement, from an outcome, such as people reaching the intended support more reliably. Explain how the proposed activity is expected to contribute to that change and what must also hold true. This explanation is a working theory of change, not proof that the activity will produce the outcome. [S2, S1]",
"Develop more than one reasonable option before selecting a preferred response. Options may differ in access, disruption, operating demands, resources and the authority required. Include the possibility that an existing arrangement can be adjusted rather than assuming a new structure is necessary. Ask affected people which trade-offs matter and seek specialist advice for technical questions. A climate adaptation proposal should not be justified solely by its environmental label. It should explain the concern being addressed, the proposed contribution and the information needed to check that contribution. [S2, R2]",
"Consider whether an option shifts a burden or creates a new risk. A protected facility may remain unusable if the approach route is inaccessible; a service change may transfer time or cost to households. The IPCC assessment identifies maladaptation as a relevant concern, while local consequences require actual investigation. Do not recommend relocation, construction or ecological alteration without the necessary authority and expertise. Record how livelihood, relationships, service access and the people whose views were missing will enter the comparison, rather than treating physical completion as a sufficient outcome. [S2, R8]",
"Use a simple options note with the desired outcome, expected contribution, affected people, assumptions, trade-offs and unresolved advice. The participation review supports examining influence while the options remain open; it does not validate a scoring formula for this site. An option may remain provisional until resource or technical questions are resolved. Make this visible in the plan and explain the next decision point. If an urgent concern is identified, use the relevant response process separately instead of waiting for the whole adaptation plan to be approved. [R2, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a learning centre considers changing its contact hours or funding an accessible contact arrangement during recurring disruption. Learners compare the options against reaching support reliably. The team checks who may carry extra time or cost, and keeps the proposal provisional until the responsible service confirms feasibility.",
"Prepare an options note comparing two responses and the current arrangement. Give the desired outcome, one assumption per option, a possible transferred burden and the advice needed before selection.",
"A useful comparison links each option to the same outcome and makes trade-offs visible. It does not assume an adaptation label or a completed activity proves that access improves.",["S2","S1","R2","R8"],[
("Outputs and outcomes answer different planning questions.","A project completes a contact list. Which statement describes an outcome needing separate evidence?","People can reach the intended support more reliably.",["The list was produced.","A meeting to discuss the list occurred.","The list was saved in the approved system."],"A completed output may contribute to a change in access but does not demonstrate it."),
("A theory of change includes assumptions.","A contact arrangement is expected to improve access only if the service remains available. How should this be recorded?","As an assumption to verify and monitor.",["As a guaranteed condition requiring no check.","As proof that the arrangement has already worked.","As a detail unrelated to the intended outcome."],"The pathway depends on conditions beyond producing the arrangement."),
("Options should be compared before the choice is fixed.","A team calls a discussion co-design after removing every alternative. What should be reconsidered?","Whether people can meaningfully influence the choice and its trade-offs.",["Whether the label alone establishes shared influence.","Whether attendance replaces an options comparison.","Whether fewer explanations always make participation more meaningful."],"Meaningful involvement needs an honest account of what remains open."),
("Adaptation can transfer burdens or create new risks.","A facility is protected but its revised route adds a costly journey for some users. What needs review?","The resulting distribution of access and costs alongside the protection benefit.",["Only whether construction finished on time.","A rule that environmental projects cannot have adverse consequences.","An assumption that all users benefit equally."],"A plan must consider consequences beyond the principal output."),
("Technical feasibility requires appropriate authority and expertise.","An option would alter a drainage structure. What should an introductory social work plan state?","The need for authorised technical assessment before committing to that option.",["A final engineering design created from a workshop vote.","A construction guarantee based on a general climate report.","Permission for untrained residents to modify the structure."],"Community contribution complements, rather than replaces, the required technical decision."),
]),
m("Delivery ownership and operating resources","Turn a provisional option into a bounded delivery plan with responsible roles and explicit resource conditions.",[
"A delivery plan states what will happen, who is responsible, what authority is required and when each decision can be reviewed. Separate the role coordinating the work from roles approving expenditure, providing technical advice and responding to the community. Use specific responsibilities rather than assigning everything to 'partners'. Confirm that a proposed organisation accepts its role before describing the arrangement as agreed. The ecosocial practitioner study offers contextual questions about organisational support; it does not establish that a local service has capacity simply because the plan lists it. [R1, S1]",
"Plan for operation as well as setup. Identify the resources needed to keep the arrangement useful, including staff time, communication, accessibility, maintenance and any appropriate supervision. Distinguish confirmed resources from requests still awaiting approval. If an option depends on unpaid work, discuss the burden and whether the arrangement is fair and feasible rather than treating community commitment as unlimited capacity. The climate assessment supports attention to enabling conditions, while a local plan still needs an actual decision about responsibilities and resources. [S2, R2]",
"Use transparent arithmetic and clearly label fictional planning estimates. In a teaching example, an arrangement has thirty planning units available. Setup needs twelve units and operation needs four units per month for three months: twenty-four units in total, leaving six unallocated. These are invented exercise units, not Nigerian market prices or an authorised budget. The remaining amount must not be described as available for a new activity until the responsible role has checked contingencies, conditions and approval. A balanced total alone does not establish affordability, rights compliance or effectiveness. [S1]",
"Identify dependencies and a fallback. If the access route, staff role or operating approval remains unresolved, specify what must be confirmed before starting and how the team will communicate a delay. Explain any temporary support that can be authorised separately. Avoid promising another service's admission, funding or technical result. Retain the agreed decision, responsible roles and review point in the approved record. The IJSDS contextual study helps learners notice interacting constraints, but local delivery must test which dependency actually matters rather than assume a published pattern applies to everyone. [R8, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a contact arrangement has thirty exercise units, with twelve for setup and four monthly for three months. The coordinator checks the operating role, accessible communication cost and spending authority before calling the option ready. Six units remain unallocated; the figures are not real prices.",
"Make a delivery table with activity, owner, approving role, dependency, resource status and date. Calculate the example's total and remaining units, then identify an operating issue the arithmetic cannot resolve.",
"The exercise total is twenty-four units with six unallocated. A feasible plan also needs confirmed roles, authority and operation; a correct sum does not provide those commitments.",["R1","S1","S2","R2","R8"],[
("A named organisation is not the same as an accepted responsibility.","A plan assigns work to a partner that has not agreed. What status is accurate?","Proposed responsibility awaiting confirmation.",["A binding delivery commitment because its name appears.","Completed work because another service was identified.","A reason to omit the action owner."],"Responsibility needs acknowledgement and appropriate authority."),
("Operating resources matter alongside setup.","A budget funds installation but no ongoing staffing or maintenance. What should be added?","The operating requirements, owners and resource conditions.",["A statement that setup funding guarantees permanent operation.","An assumption that community volunteers have unlimited time.","Only another launch event to demonstrate sustainability."],"An arrangement needs resources over the period it is intended to work."),
("Fictional budget totals must include the stated operating period.","With thirty exercise units, twelve for setup and four monthly for three months, how many units remain unallocated?","Six units.",["Fourteen units.","Eighteen units.","Two units."],"Twelve plus three times four equals twenty-four, leaving six of the thirty exercise units."),
("Unallocated funds still require the relevant approval.","An exercise budget has an unallocated balance. What does that balance establish?","An arithmetic remainder, with spending conditions and approval still to check.",["Automatic permission to spend it on any activity.","Proof that the arrangement will improve outcomes.","A real Nigerian market-price estimate."],"A balance is not a substitute for authority or an assessment of the proposal."),
("Dependencies should be resolved before readiness is claimed.","A contact plan depends on an unconfirmed receiving role. What should the delivery note show?","The dependency, confirmation owner and fallback or delay communication.",["Readiness because the task table is complete.","A guarantee of admission by the receiving service.","No mention of the unresolved role."],"A plan is reviewable when outstanding conditions are explicit."),
]),
m("Monitoring review triggers and revision","Choose measures and a review trigger that match the intended outcome without claiming an untested causal effect.",[
"Monitoring asks whether the plan is being implemented and whether the intended change is occurring. Define an output measure and an outcome question separately. A count of contacts made may show activity, while users' ability to reach the relevant support addresses a different question. Establish a proportionate starting point and use consistent definitions across reviews. Avoid collecting identifiable details simply because disaggregation sounds useful. Where differences matter, use categories and reporting arrangements that protect people and explain any missing responses. [S1, S2]",
"A review trigger tells the responsible role when to examine the arrangement again. It may be a missed operating commitment, new evidence of an access barrier or an agreed review date. In a fictional pilot, two consecutive reviews with an unresolved contact barrier could trigger a joint service discussion. This is an invented management rule for the exercise, not a weather warning or a clinical threshold. Urgent safety concerns still follow the applicable response procedure immediately. Do not delay an urgent response until a routine monitoring condition has been reached. [S1, S2]",
"Look for unintended consequences and alternative explanations. An improvement after implementation may also reflect a change in weather, demand, staffing or another service. The participation review can inform who contributes to the review, but its thematic findings do not prove the effect of the local plan. Ask whether people report a meaningful change and whose experience remains absent. If a proposed measure only records expenditure or attendance, add a question that examines the relevant access or participation outcome without promising that monitoring alone will establish causation. [R2, R8]",
"Use the authorised revision process when evidence challenges the plan. State what changed, why it changed, who approved the adjustment and how contributors will be informed. Preserve the relevant earlier reasoning instead of silently replacing it with a success story. A plan may need to continue, change, pause a specific activity or seek further expertise, depending on the evidence and authority. Reflection on organisational conditions can help identify the role able to act; it does not make an unsupported promise of capacity or remove responsibility for responding to the current concern. [R1, S1]"],
"Fictional case: contacts increase during a pilot, but users still report an inaccessible next step. The agreed review trigger prompts a joint discussion. The team checks the pathway and changing service demand, records an authorised adjustment and avoids presenting the contact count as proof of improved wellbeing.",
"Write a monitoring note with one output measure, one outcome question, a baseline source, a non-emergency review trigger and a revision owner. Give two alternative explanations for an observed improvement.",
"Strong monitoring matches the intended change and gives a route to respond. It separates management triggers from urgent safety procedures and avoids attributing causation from a simple before-and-after observation.",["S1","S2","R2","R8","R1"],[
("An outcome question should match the intended participation change.","A plan aims to improve access but measures only spending. What should the review add?","A proportionate question about whether intended users can reach support.",["A claim that spending proves access improved.","A larger publicity count unrelated to the barrier.","An assumption that no feedback is needed."],"A relevant outcome question examines the intended change."),
("A management review trigger is distinct from a safety threshold.","A pilot uses two unresolved review points as a discussion trigger. How should this rule be described?","An agreed exercise management rule that does not delay urgent procedures.",["A universal medical emergency threshold.","A scientific weather-warning limit.","Permission to ignore immediate concerns until two reviews occur."],"The rule's purpose and limits should remain explicit."),
("Improvement after implementation can have other explanations.","Service access improves after a pilot while staffing also changes. What should the report consider?","The staffing change and other plausible influences before attributing the effect.",["Timing alone as complete causal proof.","Only the explanation preferred by the project team.","A thematic review as a controlled test of this pilot."],"Monitoring observations do not isolate the intervention's effect by themselves."),
("Revisions should preserve the reason and approval history.","A monitoring finding leads to a changed plan. What should the record retain?","The finding, reason, approving role and communication of the adjustment.",["Only the newest version with no explanation.","An invented claim that the original plan always included the change.","A public list of identifiable complainants."],"A transparent history supports accountability and future learning."),
("Participation in evaluation should include challenging feedback.","A review receives a report that contradicts its success narrative. What is appropriate?","Examine the report and its evidence through the agreed review process.",["Exclude the report because it complicates the launch story.","Assume every critical account proves total failure.","Replace the account with an average without investigation."],"A useful review remains open to evidence that changes its conclusion."),
]),
])
